"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"][14]={"university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M14-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M14-L1 – Perceptron and multilayer networks<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M14 – Bachelor: Neural networks and introduction to deep learning (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M9, M10.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain linear decision\nboundaries, construct a non-linear network architecture and count\nparameters.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A decision based on weighted features<\/h2>\n<p>A conveyor system should send a workpiece for further inspection only\nif exactly one of two inspection sensors is triggered. The sensor states\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>∈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1,x_2\\in\\{0,1\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare dimensionless. A single threshold unit first computes a score\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒘<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒙<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=\\mathbf w^\\top\\mathbf x+b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand reports\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\notherwise\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe classical perceptron is a model of this kind; its learning rule\ncorrects an incorrect decision using\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒘<\/mi><mo>←<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒘<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒙<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf w\\leftarrow\\mathbf w+\\eta(y_{\\mathrm{soll}}-y)\\mathbf x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>←<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b\\leftarrow b+\\eta(y_{\\mathrm{soll}}-y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith learning rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe subscript ‘soll’ denotes the target value. For finite, linearly\nseparable training data, the classical rule converges under the usual\nassumptions; there is no such guarantee for arbitrary data.<\/p>\n<p>The system’s requirement corresponds to XOR. For (0,0) and (1,1) the\nscore must be non-positive; for (1,0) and (0,1) it must be positive.\nThis implies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b\\le0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_1+b&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_2+b&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_1+w_2+b\\le0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHowever, the two middle inequalities imply\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_1+w_2+b&gt;-b\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA contradiction: a straight line cannot separate the four combinations\nas required.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A hidden layer for feature construction<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001738_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Drei Kurven zeigen die beiden gewichteten ReLU-Aktivierungen und ihre Differenz; die Wahrheitstabelle zeigt 0, 1, 1, 0.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Drei Kurven zeigen die beiden gewichteten\nReLU-Aktivierungen und ihre Differenz; die Wahrheitstabelle zeigt 0, 1,\n1, 0.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: XOR aus zwei ReLU-Einheiten.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Instead of a single unit, we use two ReLU units. ReLU denotes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r(z)=\\max(0,z)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSet\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=x_1+x_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_1=r(s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_2=r(s-1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe output\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>o<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o=h_1-2h_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyields exactly\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0,1,0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=0,1,2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nrespectively. Further inspection is triggered at an output threshold of\n0.5 or above. The illustration shows this decomposition: the second unit\nreduces the previously increasing score when both sensors are\nactive.<\/p>\n<p>In general, a fully connected layer with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninputs and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\noutputs is described by a matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W\\in\\mathbb R^{m\\times d}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a bias vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b\\in\\mathbb R^m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m(d+1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntrainable scalars. The network 2→2→1 therefore has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2(2+1)+1(2+1)=9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nparameters, even if individual values in the constructed example are\nidentical. Without a non-linear intermediate operation, any composition\nof affine layers would again reduce to an affine map:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_2(W_1x+b_1)+b_2=(W_2W_1)x+(W_2b_1+b_2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Checking our own construction<\/h2>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> itertools <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> product<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> x1, x2 <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> product((<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>), repeat<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    s <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> x1 <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> x2<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    h1, h2 <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, s), <span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, s<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">int<\/span>(h1<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>h2 <span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.5<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> (x1 <span class=\"op\">^<\/span> x2)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Here the weights were chosen algebraically, not trained. This is\npossible for small, known rules. In many applications, however, we have\nexamples but no manageable decision formula. There the weights are\nadjusted through optimisation. An architecture provides a set of\npossible functions; its expressiveness alone tells us nothing about the\neffort of learning or generalisation. Real sensor data are also noisy,\nand a hard threshold can switch frequently in borderline cases.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>How many parameters does a fully connected network 4→6→3→1 have, with\nbiases in every computational layer? Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>55<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6\\cdot5+3\\cdot7+1\\cdot4=55<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf the two hidden layers use identity functions and the output is also\naffine, the entire network can be replaced by a single affine map from\nfour values to one. Explain why more parameters alone cannot create a\nnon-linear decision boundary: it is the non-linear transformation that\nchanges the function class.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://d2l.ai/chapter_multilayer-perceptrons/mlp.html\">Zhang et\nal.: Dive into Deep Learning, §5.1<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – affine and non-linear layers,\nselective comparison. <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://courses.cis.cornell.edu/cs4780/2023sp/lectures/lecturenote03.html\">Cornell\nUniversity, CS4780: The Perceptron (Spring 2023)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – convergence\ntheorem; ±1 convention. <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M14-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M14-L2 – Activation functions and forward computation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M14 – Bachelor: Neural networks and introduction to deep learning (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M9, M10, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M14-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Select activations, track tensor\nshapes and calculate a multiclass prediction numerically.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From score to activation<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001506_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Kurven für ReLU, Sigmoid und Tanh von −4 bis 4 auf gemeinsamer Skala.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Kurven für ReLU, Sigmoid und Tanh von −4\nbis 4 auf gemeinsamer Skala.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Drei Aktivierungsfunktionen.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A network for classifying the condition of a pump receives two\nalready scaled measurements. An intermediate neuron first produces a\nweighted sum. An activation function transforms this into a new value.\nFor real-valued scores\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthree important possibilities are<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>σ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">tanh<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><mrow><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r(z)=\\max(0,z),\\qquad \\sigma(z)=\\frac{1}{1+e^{-z}},\\qquad \\tanh(z)=\\frac{e^z-e^{-z}}{e^z+e^{-z}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>ReLU leaves positive values unchanged, sets negative values to zero\nand is not classically differentiable at zero. Backpropagation uses a\nconvention there (often 0). Sigmoid yields values between 0 and 1, and\ntanh between −1 and 1. Their derivatives are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>σ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>σ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma&#39;(z)=\\sigma(z)(1-\\sigma(z))<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">tanh<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">tanh<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tanh&#39;(z)=1-\\tanh^2(z)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor large absolute values these become very small; the illustration\nshows the saturation. Sigmoid is common for a binary output with an\nappropriate loss, and ReLU for hidden layers; there is no universally\noptimal activation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two layers by hand<\/h2>\n<p>We use column vectors. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=(1,2)^\\top<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_1=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;-1\\\\2&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b_1=(0,-1)^\\top<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_1=(-1,3)^\\top<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=r(z_1)=(0,3)^\\top<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn output layer with three rows\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_2=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\0&amp;1\\\\-1&amp;-1\\end{pmatrix}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b_2=(1,-1,2)^\\top<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nproduces the logits\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>o<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o=(1,2,-1)^\\top<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe categories are mutually exclusive: normal, monitor, shut down. The\nsoftmax function couples the outputs:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>o<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/munder><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>o<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/munder><msub><mi>o<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_i=\\frac{\\exp(o_i-m)}{\\sum_j\\exp(o_j-m)},\\qquad m=\\max_j o_j.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Subtracting the maximum leaves the ratio unchanged and avoids\noverflow for very large positive scores. Here the denominator is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.4177<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-1}+1+e^{-3}\\approx1.4177<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.2595<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.7054<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.0351<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p\\approx(0.2595,0.7054,0.0351)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe model’s decision by the maximum is to monitor. These values are\nnormalised as probabilities within the model but are not automatically\nempirically calibrated.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Shapes and batch computation<\/h2>\n<p>In a matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nexample rows, the same layer computes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><msup><mi>W<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z=XW^\\top+b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith output shape\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(B,m)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe bias is added to each row. Softmax must operate across categories\nfor each example, not across examples. Multiple independent binary\noutputs for faults that may occur simultaneously would instead require\nseparate sigmoid functions and a corresponding loss.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>x <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>w1 <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([[<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>, <span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>], [<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>w2 <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([[<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>], [<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>], [<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>, <span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>h <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.maximum(w1 <span class=\"op\">@<\/span> x <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> [<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>, <span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>], <span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>o <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> w2 <span class=\"op\">@<\/span> h <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> [<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>, <span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>e <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.exp(o<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>o.<span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>())<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>p <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> e<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>e.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>()<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(o, [<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, <span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(p, [<span class=\"fl\">0.259496<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">0.705385<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">0.035119<\/span>], atol<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-6<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(p.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(), <span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>A forward computation does not yet change the weights. Learning\ninformation is calculated only from the target state, model value and\nloss. Before implementation, it is useful to write down the shape of\nevery intermediate matrix; transposed weights or the wrong softmax axis\ncan then be detected early.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>What happens when 1000 is added to all three logits? Solution:\nsoftmax remains unchanged because the same factor cancels in the\nnumerator and denominator. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nReLU is 0, sigmoid is approximately 0.1192 and tanh is approximately\n−0.9640. Which function yields negative values while remaining bounded?\nTanh.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://d2l.ai/chapter_multilayer-perceptrons/mlp.html\">Zhang et\nal.: Dive into Deep Learning, §5.1<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – activations.\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://d2l.ai/chapter_multilayer-perceptrons/backprop.html\">Zhang\net al.: Dive into Deep Learning, §5.3<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – forward computation.\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://d2l.ai/chapter_linear-classification/softmax-regression.html\">Zhang\net al.: Dive into Deep Learning, 4.1 Softmax Regression<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\nnormalisation by softmax. <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M14-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M14-L3 – Chain rule and backpropagation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M14 – Bachelor: Neural networks and introduction to deep learning (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M9, M10, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M14-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive gradients in a small\ncomputational graph and check them using finite differences.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Composing local derivatives<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001516_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Vier Rechenoperationen verbinden Eingabe und Verlust; rückwärts fließen Ableitungen bis zu den Gewichten.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Vier Rechenoperationen verbinden Eingabe\nund Verlust; rückwärts fließen Ableitungen bis zu den\nGewichten.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Vorwärtswerte und Rückwärtsgradienten.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>When calibrating a measurement, the model output should equal 5. We\nuse a scalar network with two weights\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w,v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a fixed input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe graph is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=wx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=r(a)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>o<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o=vh<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>o<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=\\tfrac12(o-y)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=h=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>o<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAll numbers in this teaching example are scaled and dimensionless. The\nloss value has no direction; to make a correction we need derivatives\nwith respect to the parameters.<\/p>\n<p>The chain rule allows a longer dependency path to be evaluated as a\nproduct of local derivatives. The backward computation (backpropagation)\nuses the intermediate values stored during the forward computation. The\nstarting point is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial L/\\partial L=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe illustration associates each node with its forward value and the\nderivative propagated backwards.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Derivatives by hand<\/h2>\n<p>We have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>o<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial L/\\partial o=o-y=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt follows that<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>o<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>o<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial v}=(o-y)h=-2,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial h}=(o-y)v=-2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Since\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r&#39;(a)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial L/\\partial a=-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial L/\\partial w=(\\partial L/\\partial a)x=-4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith learning rate 0.1 and a simultaneous update of both weights, we\nobtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w&#39;=1.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v&#39;=2.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe new output is 6.16 and the loss is 0.6728. The step increased the\nloss despite correct derivatives! With learning rate 0.02, we instead\nobtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.08<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w&#39;=1.08<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.04<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v&#39;=2.04<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>o<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.4064<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o&#39;=4.4064<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.17618<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L&#39;\\approx0.17618<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA gradient provides local directional information, not a guarantee for\nan arbitrarily large step.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Gradients of a dense layer<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>W<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=Wh+b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nactivation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=\\phi(z)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand incoming gradient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=\\partial L/\\partial a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(as a column vector), let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo>⊙<\/mo><msup><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta=g\\odot\\phi&#39;(z)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>W<\/mi><\/msub><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>W<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_W L=\\delta h^\\top,\\quad \\nabla_b L=\\delta,\\quad \\nabla_h L=W^\\top\\delta.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The outer product has the shape of the weight matrix. If an\nintermediate value has several successors, their gradient contributions\nare added. For a mean batch loss the individual contributions must be\naveraged accordingly. Automatic differentiation applies such rules to\nthe operations actually performed; it is neither symbolic manipulation\nof the entire network formula nor a finite-difference approximation.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> loss(w, v):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.5<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(v<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>w)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>w, v, eps <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">1e-5<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>dw <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (loss(w<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>eps, v)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>loss(w<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>eps, v))<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>eps)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>dv <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (loss(w, v<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>eps)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>loss(w, v<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>eps))<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>eps)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(dw<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1e-7<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(dv<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1e-7<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(loss(<span class=\"fl\">1.4<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">2.2<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">0.6728<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1e-10<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> loss(<span class=\"fl\">1.08<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">2.04<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> loss(w, v)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The central finite-difference quotient is used here to debug two\nparameters. It would be very expensive for millions of parameters and\ncan differ from the implemented derivative convention at a ReLU kink.\nGradient checks therefore use smooth points and several step sizes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhile keeping\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x,v,y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nunchanged. What are the two gradients? Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>o<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=o=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=12.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe gradient with respect to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncontains the factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthat with respect to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>w<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncontains the factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r&#39;(a)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth vanish despite a large loss. This example shows why initialisation\nand activation choice matter.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://d2l.ai/chapter_multilayer-perceptrons/backprop.html\">Zhang\net al.: Dive into Deep Learning, §5.3<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – computational graph and\nbackpropagation. <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M14-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M14-L4 – Initialisation, normalisation and optimisers<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M14 – Bachelor: Neural networks and introduction to deep learning (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M9, M10, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M14-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain symmetry breaking and\nvariance scaling, apply batch normalisation and calculate an Adam\nstep.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Signals at the start of learning<\/h2>\n<p>At the beginning of training, the weights have not yet been adapted\nto the task. If all units of a hidden layer are initialised identically\nand receive identical gradients, they remain identical under a\ndeterministic update; additional units then provide no distinct\nfeatures. Small independent random values can break this symmetry. The\nbiases may nevertheless be zero.<\/p>\n<p>For independent centred inputs with variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand independent centred weights with variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nan affine neuron without bias has variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d s^2 q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes the input width. If the factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ds^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis substantially smaller or larger than one in many layers, signals may\nbecome weak or excessively large. The same difficulty can arise for\ngradients. A common Glorot initialisation sets\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s^2=2/(d+m)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor input width\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand output width\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor ReLU, He scaling\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s^2=2/d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a common alternative: under a symmetric distribution, approximately\nhalf of the second moment is lost by setting negative values to zero. It\nis an approximation, not a stability guarantee. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>64<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>128<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=64,m=128<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe standard deviations are approximately 0.1021 and 0.1768,\nrespectively.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Normalisation within the network<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001518_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Zwei Zahlenstrahlen zeigen 2, 4, 6, 8 und die zentrierten, skalierten Werte −1,342 bis 1,342.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Zwei Zahlenstrahlen zeigen 2, 4, 6, 8 und\ndie zentrierten, skalierten Werte −1,342 bis 1,342.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Batch-Normalisierung eines Merkmals.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For a feature, batch normalisation calculates the following\nquantities from the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncurrent training values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>B<\/mi><\/mfrac><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>B<\/mi><\/mfrac><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mover><mi>z<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_B=\\frac1B\\sum_i z_i,\\qquad v_B=\\frac1B\\sum_i(z_i-\\mu_B)^2,\\qquad \\tilde z_i=\\gamma\\frac{z_i-\\mu_B}{\\sqrt{v_B+\\varepsilon}}+\\beta.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nprevents division by zero;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma,\\beta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare trainable scale and shift parameters. For the values 2, 4, 6, 8, the\nmean is 5 and the variance is 5. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=1,\\beta=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand very small\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe results are approximately −1.342, −0.447, 0.447 and 1.342. The\nillustration shows the change of scale. The usual inference variant uses\nrunning estimates of training statistics, not the statistics of each\nincoming set of measurements to be predicted. Very small batches may\nprovide unreliable estimates. In contrast, layer normalisation computes\nstatistics within an example along specified feature dimensions. Both\nmethods also differ from external scaling of raw data in M3.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From gradient to update<\/h2>\n<p>In a simple SGD step,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchanges by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>η<\/mi><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\eta g_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMomentum smooths the sequence of stochastic gradients. Adam additionally\nuses a running estimate of squared gradients. For initial values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_0=v_0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le\\beta_1,\\beta_2&lt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe following holds elementwise:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msubsup><mi>g<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_t=\\beta_1m_{t-1}+(1-\\beta_1)g_t,\\quad v_t=\\beta_2v_{t-1}+(1-\\beta_2)g_t^2,<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>m<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msubsup><mi>β<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/msubsup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msubsup><mi>β<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/msubsup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><mfrac><msub><mover><mi>m<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/msqrt><mo>+<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat m_t=\\frac{m_t}{1-\\beta_1^t},\\quad\\hat v_t=\\frac{v_t}{1-\\beta_2^t},\\quad\\theta_t=\\theta_{t-1}-\\eta\\frac{\\hat m_t}{\\sqrt{\\hat v_t}+\\varepsilon}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a second raw moment, not a centred variance. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_1=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_0=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.01<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=0.01<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_1=0.9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.999<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_2=0.999<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_1=0.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.016<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_1=0.016<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith bias-corrected values 4 and 16. With very small\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe update gives approximately 1.99. SGD with the same learning rate\nwould give 1.96; the steps cannot be judged better or worse simply by\ntheir size.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>z <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">4.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">6.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">8.<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>a <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (z<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>z.mean())<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>np.sqrt(z.var()<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-8<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(a.mean(), <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.isclose(a.var(), <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>g, b1, b2 <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">4.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">0.9<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">0.999<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>m, v <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>b1)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>g, (<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>b2)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>g<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>g<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>new <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">0.01<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(m<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>b1))<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(np.sqrt(v<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>b2))<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-8<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(new, <span class=\"fl\">1.99<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(np.sqrt(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">192<\/span>), <span class=\"fl\">0.10206207<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The optimiser, learning rate and batch size belong in the experiment\nprotocol. Exploding gradients can be diagnosed and, if appropriate,\nclipped; clipping does not replace the search for faulty data or network\noperations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>A feature is a constant 7 throughout the batch. What does batch\nnormalisation produce? Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_B=7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_B=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\neach centred value is 0; the output is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>β<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor all examples. A constant input gains no new differences through this\noperation. What happens when the input width doubles under He\ninitialisation? The variance halves and the standard deviation decreases\nby a factor of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\sqrt2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://d2l.ai/chapter_multilayer-perceptrons/numerical-stability-and-init.html\">Zhang\net al.: Dive into Deep Learning, §5.4<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – initialisation.\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://d2l.ai/chapter_convolutional-modern/batch-norm.html\">Zhang\net al.: Dive into Deep Learning, §8.5<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – normalisation.\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://d2l.ai/chapter_optimization/adam.html\">Zhang et\nal.: Dive into Deep Learning, §12.10<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – Adam.\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M14-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M14-L5 – Regularisation, dropout and early stopping<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M14 – Bachelor: Neural networks and introduction to deep learning (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M9, M10, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M14-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Diagnose overfitting and\ndistinguish three regularisation mechanisms.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why training loss is not enough<\/h2>\n<p>A quality control system learns from historical measurements,\nincluding random disturbances. A highly flexible network may also fit\nthe random components. What matters, however, is performance on new\nworkpieces. Data from later production batches are therefore separated\nbefore training as validation and test data if this reflects subsequent\ndeployment. The validation set is used for model selection; the test set\nremains untouched until the final evaluation. A decreasing training\ncurve does not prove improvement on unseen data.<\/p>\n<p>An L2 penalty augments the mean training loss\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nfor example by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda\\|w\\|_2^2/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>w<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncomprises the weights to be regularised; biases are excluded in this\nexample. The additional derivative is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda w<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith simple SGD the update becomes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><\/msub><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w&#39;=(1-\\eta\\lambda)w-\\eta\\nabla_w L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe additional penalty is 0.9 and its gradient is 0.6. With adaptive\noptimisers, weight decay decoupled from the loss gradient (as in AdamW,\nfor example) is not generally equivalent to this L2 penalty.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Randomly dropping units<\/h2>\n<p>Dropout changes activations during training. Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe the dropout probability with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le p&lt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe a Bernoulli random variable with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(M=1)=1-p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nInverted dropout is defined by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>h<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tilde h=Mh/(1-p)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>h<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E(\\tilde h\\mid h)=h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>h<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>p<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}(\\tilde h\\mid h)=h^2p/(1-p)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=6,p=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe possible values are 0 (25%) and 8 (75%); the mean remains 6 and the\nvariance is 12. This does not guarantee that the expected value of the\nentire non-linear network remains unchanged. In the usual evaluation\nmode, dropout is disabled. The strength of the method must be selected\nusing validation data; some architectures do not require dropout at\nall.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Early stopping using a curve<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001555_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Trainingsverlust fällt fortlaufend; Validierungsverlust erreicht in Epoche vier 0,34 und steigt danach; Epoche sieben ist gestrichelt.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Trainingsverlust fällt fortlaufend;\nValidierungsverlust erreicht in Epoche vier 0,34 und steigt danach;\nEpoche sieben ist gestrichelt.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Frühes Stoppen anhand des Validierungsverlusts.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The illustration contains deliberately constructed loss values for\nseven epochs. An epoch corresponds to one complete pass through the\ntraining data; it is not the same as a single batch update. The training\nloss falls from 0.84 to 0.18. The validation loss falls to 0.34 at epoch\n4 and then rises to 0.46. With a patience of two epochs without strict\nimprovement and a minimum improvement of zero, the process stops after\nepoch 6. The stored weights from epoch 4 are restored. Epoch 7 merely\nillustrates a possible continuation. For very noisy curves, a patience\nof two may be too short.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>v <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [<span class=\"fl\">0.89<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">0.57<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">0.42<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">0.34<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">0.36<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">0.40<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">0.46<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>best, stale, best_epoch <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">float<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&#39;inf&#39;<\/span>), <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">None<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> epoch, value <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">enumerate<\/span>(v, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> value <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> best:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        best, stale, best_epoch <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> value, <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, epoch<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        stale <span class=\"op\">+=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> stale <span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">break<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> (best_epoch, epoch) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>rng <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.random.default_rng(<span class=\"dv\">54<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>a <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">6<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(rng.random(<span class=\"dv\">100_000<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.25<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"fl\">0.75<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(a.mean()<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.08<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(a.var()<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.3<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>For a reproducible comparison, all candidates should use the same\ndata split; with stochastic training, several initialisations are\nhelpful. A large difference between training and validation error can\nalso indicate distribution shift, data leakage during training or\ninappropriate preprocessing. Regularisation does not replace this\ninvestigation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>With dropout at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand activation 4, the positive output is 8. Calculate the mean and\nvariance. Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\cdot0.5+8\\cdot0.5=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe variance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>16<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5(0-4)^2+0.5(8-4)^2=16<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhy must the best epoch not be chosen using the final test set? Because\nit would then become part of model selection and would no longer provide\nan independent performance assessment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://d2l.ai/chapter_multilayer-perceptrons/dropout.html\">Zhang\net al.: Dive into Deep Learning, §5.6<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – Dropout.\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://d2l.ai/chapter_multilayer-perceptrons/generalization-deep.html\">Zhang\net al.: Dive into Deep Learning, §5.5<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – generalisation,\nsupplementary. <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M14-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M14-L6 – Overview of convolutional and sequence networks<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M14 – Bachelor: Neural networks and introduction to deep learning (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M9, M10, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M14-L5<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain weight sharing in local\nfilters and recurrent states and calculate small examples.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Data structure as an architectural choice<\/h2>\n<p>A fully connected network initially treats each position as a\nseparate feature. In an image, an edge may occur in many locations; in a\nsequence of measurements, the same local dynamics may recur. An\narchitecture can exploit this structure by using the same weights at\ndifferent spatial or temporal positions. This reduces the parameter\ncount compared with dense connectivity. However, the assumption that the\nsame operation is appropriate at every position is itself a modelling\nassumption.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Local filters in convolutional networks<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001574_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Hervorgehobenes Fenster einer 3×4-Matrix, 2×2-Kernel und mittleres Ausgabeelement 6.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Hervorgehobenes Fenster einer 3×4-Matrix,\n2×2-Kernel und mittleres Ausgabeelement 6.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Lokaler Filter für eine vertikale Kante.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For a single-channel image\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a kernel\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof size\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>×<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_h\\times k_w<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nmany deep learning libraries use cross-correlation (under the name\nconvolution):<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munderover><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munderover><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y_{i,j}=b+\\sum_{u=0}^{k_h-1}\\sum_{v=0}^{k_w-1}K_{u,v}X_{i+u,j+v}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Without padding and with stride 1, an input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi>W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H\\times W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nshrinks to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>×<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>W<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(H-k_h+1)\\times(W-k_w+1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLet a 3×4 teaching image be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>4<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>4<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>5<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>5<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>3<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>3<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1&amp;4&amp;4\\\\2&amp;2&amp;5&amp;5\\\\0&amp;0&amp;3&amp;3\\end{pmatrix}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the kernel\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=\\begin{pmatrix}-1&amp;1\\\\-1&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith bias 0, we obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>6<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>6<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;6&amp;0\\\\0&amp;6&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe kernel responds to the transition between the left and right halves\nof the image. The illustration shows the central window and its\ncontribution. A trained convolutional layer can contain many different\nfilters; with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_{in}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninput and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi>o<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_{out}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\noutput channels, dense channel connectivity requires\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi>o<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_{out}(C_{in}k_hk_w+1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nparameters. Pooling summarises neighbourhoods but may lose spatial\ndetail. Under appropriate boundary and stride conditions, convolution\nprovides translation equivariance, not automatically complete position\ninvariance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Recurrent processing of measurement sequences<\/h2>\n<p>A simple recurrent network stores a hidden state\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>o<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_t=\\phi(W_xx_t+W_hh_{t-1}+b),\\qquad o_t=W_oh_t+c.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The matrices are shared across all time steps. In the scalar teaching\nmodel we deliberately use the identity rather than a non-linear\nactivation:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_t=0.5h_{t-1}+x_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor the sequence 2, 0, 1, the resulting states are 2, 1, 1.5. An impulse\ntherefore persists briefly as a decaying contribution. When training a\nrecurrent network, the chain rule is applied through the computation\nunrolled in time. Long paths can attenuate or amplify gradients; LSTM\nand GRU address this difficulty, among others. Attention-based networks\noffer another way to connect sequence positions; detailed architectures\nfollow in the Master’s advanced modules.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> numpy.lib.stride_tricks <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> sliding_window_view<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>x <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>], [<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>], [<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>k <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>], [<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>y <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (sliding_window_view(x, k.shape)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>k).<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(axis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.array_equal(y, [[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>h, states <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>, []<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> value <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> [<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    h <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.5<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>h <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> value<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    states.append(h)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> states <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> [<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.5<\/span>]<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>A convolutional layer with 3 input and 16 output channels uses 3×3\nkernels with one bias per output channel. Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>16<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>448<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">16(3\\cdot9+1)=448<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nparameters. What is the output size for a 28×28 input without padding\nand with stride 1? 26×26 per output channel. How does the scalar\nrecurrent state of 1.5 change with two additional zero inputs? It falls\nto 0.75 and then 0.375. The history does not disappear immediately but\nloses influence.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://d2l.ai/chapter_convolutional-neural-networks/conv-layer.html\">Zhang\net al.: Dive into Deep Learning, §7.2<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – cross-correlation.\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://d2l.ai/chapter_recurrent-neural-networks/rnn.html\">Zhang\net al.: Dive into Deep Learning, §9.4<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – recurrent state.\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M14-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M14-L7 – Implementing a small neural network<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M14 – Bachelor: Neural networks and introduction to deep learning (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M9, M10, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M14-L6<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Train a network using NumPy,\nmatch gradients to parameter shapes and evaluate it on independent\ndata.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Learning task and data split<\/h2>\n<p>In a synthetic sorting system, two scaled measurements between −1 and\n1 are recorded. The label is 1 if both measurements have the same sign,\notherwise 0. Simple linear classifiers cannot correctly separate these\nfour quadrants. We use a fully connected network with two inputs, eight\ntanh units and one binary output. The data are generated independently\nfrom a uniform random model; they do not represent a real installation.\nThe random generator is initialised once for reproducibility. The first\n600 examples are used for training, the next 200 for validation and\nanother 200 for the final test. Neither training nor selection of the\nbest model variant is performed on the test data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Forward computation and loss<\/h2>\n<p>In this lesson we store examples as rows and weights in the shape\n(input width, output width). With input matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe computations are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">tanh<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=\\tanh(XW_1+b_1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z=HW_2+b_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe sigmoid output is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>σ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=\\sigma(Z)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBinary cross-entropy can be computed from the logit\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin a numerically stable way:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>B<\/mi><\/mfrac><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>B<\/mi><\/munderover><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=\\frac1B\\sum_{i=1}^B\\bigl(\\log(1+e^{z_i})-y_i z_i\\bigr).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The derivative vector of the logits is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=(P-Y)/B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBecause the loss is already averaged, the subsequent matrix product must\nnot divide by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nagain. It follows that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/msub><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>H<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_{W_2}L=H^\\top D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/msub><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_{b_2}L=\\sum_i D_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand for the first layer\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><msubsup><mi>W<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⊙<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>H<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=(DW_2^\\top)\\odot(1-H^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/msub><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mi>E<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_{W_1}L=X^\\top E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/msub><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_{b_1}L=\\sum_i E_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAll gradients are calculated using the same old weights. The update may\ntake place only afterwards.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A complete small training run<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001658_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Gemessene Trainings- und Validierungskreuzentropie sinken während 1800 Vollbatch-Updates; Testgenauigkeit 97 Prozent.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Gemessene Trainings- und\nValidierungskreuzentropie sinken während 1800 Vollbatch-Updates;\nTestgenauigkeit 97 Prozent.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Lernkurven des selbst implementierten Netzes.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The following code uses only NumPy. Full-batch gradients simplify\nchecking; large networks usually use mini-batches and specialised\nlibraries. The loss is calculated stably with <code>logaddexp<\/code>;\nthe sigmoid function is also evaluated without potential exponential\noverflow. Training and validation losses are measured before the first\nupdate and after selected updates.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>rng <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.random.default_rng(<span class=\"dv\">314<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>x <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> rng.uniform(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, size<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1000<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>y <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> ((x[:, <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>x[:, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]) <span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>).astype(<span class=\"bu\">float<\/span>)[:, <span class=\"va\">None<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>xt, yt <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> x[:<span class=\"dv\">600<\/span>], y[:<span class=\"dv\">600<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>xv, yv <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> x[<span class=\"dv\">600<\/span>:<span class=\"dv\">800<\/span>], y[<span class=\"dv\">600<\/span>:<span class=\"dv\">800<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>xe, ye <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> x[<span class=\"dv\">800<\/span>:], y[<span class=\"dv\">800<\/span>:]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>w1 <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> rng.normal(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">0.6<\/span>, (<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>))<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> b1 <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.zeros(<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>w2 <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> rng.normal(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">0.5<\/span>, (<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>))<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> b2 <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.zeros(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> forward(a):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    h <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.tanh(a <span class=\"op\">@<\/span> w1 <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> b1)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    z <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> h <span class=\"op\">@<\/span> w2 <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> b2<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    p <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.exp(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>np.logaddexp(<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>, <span class=\"op\">-<\/span>z))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> h, z, p<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> loss(a, target):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    z <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> forward(a)[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> np.mean(np.logaddexp(<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>, z)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>target<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>z)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>history <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, loss(xt, yt), loss(xv, yv))]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> step <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">1801<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    h, z, p <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> forward(xt)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-24\"><a href=\"#cb1-24\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    d <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (p<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>yt)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(yt)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-25\"><a href=\"#cb1-25\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    e <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (d <span class=\"op\">@<\/span> w2.T)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>h<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>h)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-26\"><a href=\"#cb1-26\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    dw2, db2 <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> h.T <span class=\"op\">@<\/span> d, d.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(axis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-27\"><a href=\"#cb1-27\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    dw1, db1 <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> xt.T <span class=\"op\">@<\/span> e, e.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(axis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-28\"><a href=\"#cb1-28\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    w1 <span class=\"op\">-=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.15<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>dw1<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> b1 <span class=\"op\">-=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.15<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>db1<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-29\"><a href=\"#cb1-29\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    w2 <span class=\"op\">-=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.15<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>dw2<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> b2 <span class=\"op\">-=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.15<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>db2<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-30\"><a href=\"#cb1-30\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> step <span class=\"op\">%<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">200<\/span> <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-31\"><a href=\"#cb1-31\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        history.append((step, loss(xt, yt), loss(xv, yv)))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-32\"><a href=\"#cb1-32\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>accuracy <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.mean((forward(xe)[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>] <span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.5<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> ye)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-33\"><a href=\"#cb1-33\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&#39;Train/Valid:&#39;<\/span>, history[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>], history[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-34\"><a href=\"#cb1-34\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&#39;Test accuracy:&#39;<\/span>, accuracy)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-35\"><a href=\"#cb1-35\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> history[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>][<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>] <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> history[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>][<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-36\"><a href=\"#cb1-36\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> accuracy <span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.90<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The curves in the illustration come from this run and use the same\nloss definition. The training loss decreases from 0.7468 to 0.0920 and\nthe validation loss from 0.7900 to 0.1143. In the final test, 194 of 200\npoints are correctly classified (97%). The test accuracy applies only to\nindependent points from the assumed distribution; it would not be\nguaranteed for differently distributed measurements or noisy labels.\nPoints near the coordinate axes are especially sensitive to measurement\nnoise. For real-world approval, error types, uncertainty, robustness and\noperating costs would also need to be investigated. The architecture has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>33<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8(2+1)+1(8+1)=33<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nlearnable parameters. Rather than requiring millions of parameters, this\ndeliberately small task uses an appropriate non-linear model class.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>What are the shapes of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/msub><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D,E,\\nabla_{W_1}L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor 600 training examples? Solution: (600,1), (600,8) and (2,8). Why is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot updated before calculating\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?\nOtherwise the derivative of the first layer would no longer come from\nthe same forward graph. As an additional experiment, the number of\nhidden units can be reduced to 1; compare only the training and\nvalidation curves. An independent, previously unused test set would be\nrequired for a subsequent new model selection.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://d2l.ai/chapter_multilayer-perceptrons/mlp.html\">Zhang et\nal.: Dive into Deep Learning, §5.1<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – multilayer network.\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://d2l.ai/chapter_multilayer-perceptrons/backprop.html\">Zhang\net al.: Dive into Deep Learning, §5.3<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – backpropagation; Original\nNumPy code and synthetic data. <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/2.15/generated/torch.nn.BCEWithLogitsLoss.html\">PyTorch\n2.15: BCEWithLogitsLoss<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – API reference accessed, supplementary\nreference; independent NumPy example. <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
